<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kang Youwei</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1858">1858</a>–<a href="/years/1927">1927</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kang Youwei was a Chinese political thinker and reformer of the late <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qing-empire">Qing dynasty</a>, born in Nanhai County, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangdong">Guangdong</a>, in 1858. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>'s defeat by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> in 1895 he, his student Liang Qichao and over 600 examination candidates petitioned the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a> against the Treaty of Shimonoseki, and in 1898 both took a leading part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-days-reform">Hundred Days' Reform</a>. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empress-dowager-cixi">Empress Dowager Cixi</a> ended the reform by a coup and ordered his arrest and execution, Kang fled abroad and organised the Protect the Emperor Society. He remained an advocate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constitutional-monarchy">constitutional monarchy</a>, launched a failed coup in 1917 and set out a cosmopolitan utopia in his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/datong">Datong</a> Shu. He died in Qingdao in 1927.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kang-youwei.jpg" alt="Kang Youwei" width="330" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Elmer Chickering · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kang_Yu-wei_cph.3a45114.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Many temples were destroyed in the Taiping and Boxer rebellions; in 1898 <b>Kang Youwei</b> persuaded the emperor to turn temples into schools, and after the revolution of 1911 most temples were put to other uses or destroyed.</blockquote><a href="/religion/chinese-folk-religion">Chinese folk religion · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
